% ============================================================================
% preposition-type — three named categories of preposition and what each
% actually shows (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% A preposition's JOB depends on WHICH relationship it points out -- some
% prepositions locate something in space, some place it in time, and some
% trace a path it is moving along. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? preposition_type(preposition_of_time, $D)
%       % shows_when_something_happened_or_will_happen
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `preposition_type(type, description)` carrying the source
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on a preposition-category term that is not one
% of these three. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and
% recall the type it names:
%
%     ? preposition_type($T, shows_how_something_is_moving_or_which_way_its_going)
%       % preposition_of_direction
%
% The three preposition types, as a little truth table:
%
%     type                       description                                                   quote (verbatim)
%     -------------------------  ------------------------------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     preposition_of_place       shows_where_something_is_or_where_something_happened            "Prepositions of place show where something is or where something happened."
%     preposition_of_time        shows_when_something_happened_or_will_happen                     "Prepositions of time show when something happened or will happen (and sometimes its duration)."
%     preposition_of_direction   shows_how_something_is_moving_or_which_way_its_going              "Prepositions of direction or movement show how something is moving or which way it’s going."
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Grammarly's "Prepositions: Definition,
% Types, and Examples" article, "Types of prepositions" section, which
% introduces each of these three categories in its own complete sentence
% before moving on to worked examples. The original authoring note said the
% quotes were "WebFetch-verified before writing (twice ...)"; that note is
% superseded by the raw-page measurement below, since WebFetch summaries
% are not verbatim evidence (the direction quote above had a straight
% apostrophe where the page writes U+2019). `trust consensus` — Grammarly
% is a
% reputable secondary reference, the same tier the sibling
% `noun-type.adj`/`verb-type.adj`/`pronoun-type.adj` (the other libraries in
% this directory) already use for their Grammarly citations. A preposition
% category that is NOT one of these three — "preposition_of_manner_cause_or_purpose",
% a real category the same page also names, but one that bundles THREE
% distinct functions (manner OR cause OR purpose) under a single label
% rather than one clean, single-concept category like the three tabled
% here — is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS on it rather than
% inventing a description. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
%
% EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SENTENCE (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e, #14986). The envelope
% used to be the place row's own sentence, the primary source of all three
% answers. Each quoted sentence above names its type and what it shows, so
% every row now takes its own as a `source`. The envelope is now the
% article's framing sentence, which names no type:
%
%     "However, the most common prepositions fit into four main categories,
%      with a fifth category for additional types."
%
% (It counts four main categories; the fourth, manner/cause/purpose, is the
% one deliberately not tabled, as explained above.) Measured 2026-09-15 on
% the raw page (HTTP 200; a nonsense path on the same host returns 404), with
% inline tags removed without a space and only ASCII whitespace collapsed:
% the three quotes above and the envelope each occur exactly once, each
% inside a `<p>`. The direction quote was written with a straight apostrophe
% (U+0027) in "it's"; in that form it occurred zero times, and it now
% carries the page's U+2019. Every row's page is the envelope's, so no row
% restates a `locator` line or `trust` (ADJ-TABLES §4).
% ============================================================================

table preposition_type {
    columns type, description

    row (preposition_of_place, shows_where_something_is_or_where_something_happened) {
        source "Prepositions of place show where something is or where something happened."
    }
    row (preposition_of_time, shows_when_something_happened_or_will_happen) {
        source "Prepositions of time show when something happened or will happen (and sometimes its duration)."
    }
    row (preposition_of_direction, shows_how_something_is_moving_or_which_way_its_going) {
        source "Prepositions of direction or movement show how something is moving or which way it’s going."
    }

    % THE ENVELOPE WAS THE PLACE ROW'S OWN SENTENCE, the primary source of
    % all three answers. It is now a framing sentence that names no type.
    % Every row overrides it, so it reaches no answer.
    source "However, the most common prepositions fit into four main categories, with a fifth category for additional types."
    locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/parts-of-speech/prepositions/"
    trust consensus
}
